    pr_ref:  # 6e63
    description: "Source: DEEP cohort 3 reflect on deep-20260510-054430 (ansible).\n\nansible playbooks use Jinja-templated import_tasks and include_tasks directives to compose task files. The bakeoff reflect saw ~180 cross-language edges where the destination resolves to 'unresolved:' because the Jinja template expression isn't being statically evaluated. Examples include {{ ansible_os_family }}.yml dispatched include_tasks patterns where the template variable is determined by inventory facts at runtime.\n\nPattern: jinja-template-as-import-string is unresolvable in general (the variable comes from runtime fact gathering), but a meaningful subset is resolvable as 'one of N candidate files matching the pattern in the same role's tasks/ directory'. Treating these as fan-out edges to all candidate files (with confidence reflecting the uncertainty) preserves more information than collapsing to 'unresolved:'.\n\nFix direction: in the ansible analyzer's import_tasks/include_tasks handler, when the import string contains a Jinja expression like {{ ... }}, scan the role's tasks/ for files matching the literal portions and emit fan-out edges with reduced confidence (e.g., 0.3). When no Jinja expression is present, the literal-path resolution already works.\n\nCohort 3 contains airflow + ansible; ansible scored 28/35 (well below cohort mean), and this finding is part of why. Sister observation in agent notes' 'cohort 3 REGRESSING (-14.4)' — this isn't the only cause but is the most actionable.\n\nEvidence file: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/bakeoff_artifacts/deep-20260510-054430/reflect/cohort-003/iter-001/ansible.assessment.yaml."  # 6e63
    fields: {}  # 6e63
